## 🧠 What is an Instance Method?

An **instance method** belongs to an **object (instance)** of a class.
This means every time you create a new object, that object gets access to its own version of instance variables.

Instance methods are used when your behavior depends on **object data** (like `name`, `speed`, or `balance`).

### ✅ Correct Way:

You must **create an object** to call an instance method.

```java
class Car {
    int speed; // instance variable

    void start() {  // instance method
        System.out.println("Car started at speed: " + speed);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car c1 = new Car();
        c1.speed = 80;
        c1.start();   // ✅ Correct: called using object
    }
}
```

### ❌ Wrong Way:

Calling an instance method without creating an object will cause a **compiler error**.

```java
class Car {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Car started");
    }

    static void show() {
        start();  // ❌ Wrong: cannot call instance method inside static method directly
    }
}
```

**Error:**

```
non-static method start() cannot be referenced from a static context
```

To fix it, you must create an object inside the static method:

```java
new Car().start(); // ✅ Correct
```

