## ⚠️ When Static Method Tries to Access Instance Data

Static methods **can’t access** instance variables or instance methods directly because static methods don’t belong to any object.

### ❌ Wrong Example:

```java
class Person {
    String name;

    static void printName() {
        System.out.println(name); // ❌ Error: cannot make a static reference to non-static field
    }
}
```

This fails because `name` belongs to a specific object, and `printName()` doesn’t know *which* object you are talking about.

### ✅ Correct Example:

You must create an object first to use instance members.

```java
class Person {
    String name = "Deepak";

    static void printName() {
        Person p = new Person();
        System.out.println(p.name); // ✅ Correct
    }
}
```

---

## 🔄 When Instance Method Accesses Static Data

An instance method **can** access static variables and methods, because they belong to the class, and all instances share them.

### ✅ Correct Example:

```java
class Student {
    static String schoolName = "ABC School";
    String studentName;

    void showInfo() {
        System.out.println(studentName + " studies at " + schoolName);
    }
}
```

Here `showInfo()` (instance method) can easily access `schoolName` (static variable).

