## 🧩 What Happens if You Call Static Method Using Object Reference?

Let’s test this example:

```java
class Test {
    static void show() {
        System.out.println("Static method");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t = new Test();
        t.show(); // ❌ Works but bad practice
    }
}
```

This code **compiles and runs**, but it internally calls:

```java
Test.show();
```

It **does not** depend on `t`.
So it’s misleading — you might think `t.show()` depends on the object `t`, but it doesn’t.
Therefore, **always use the class name** to call static methods.

