## ✅ **Where Method Overloading IS Supported (Valid Cases)**

Java **supports method overloading** in the following situations:

---

### 🔹 1. **Different Number of Parameters**

```java
void show() {}
void show(int a) {}
void show(int a, int b) {}
```

> ✅ Valid — different number of parameters.

---

### 🔹 2. **Different Data Types of Parameters**

```java
void display(int a) {}
void display(double a) {}
```

> ✅ Valid — same number of parameters but different types.

---

### 🔹 3. **Different Order of Parameters (Different Types)**

```java
void print(int a, String b) {}
void print(String b, int a) {}
```

> ✅ Valid — parameter types are same but order is different.

---

### 🔹 4. **Constructor Overloading**

```java
class Student {
    Student() {}
    Student(String name) {}
    Student(String name, int age) {}
}
```

> ✅ Constructor overloading is fully supported in Java.

---

## ❌ **Where Method Overloading is NOT Supported (Invalid Cases)**

Java does **not allow** overloading in these situations:

---

### 🔴 1. **Same Parameters with Different Return Type Only**

```java
int sum(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
double sum(int a, int b) { return a + b; } // ❌ Error
```

> ❌ Not valid — return type alone **does not distinguish** methods.

---

### 🔴 2. **Same Method Name and Parameters in Same Class**

```java
void test(int x) {}
void test(int x) {} // ❌ Duplicate method
```

> ❌ Not valid — compiler sees this as a **duplicate** method.

---

### 🔴 3. **Only Changing Variable Names**

```java
void calc(int a, int b) {}
void calc(int x, int y) {} // ❌ Invalid overloading
```

> ❌ Parameter **names don’t matter** for overloading — only types and number matter.

---

## 🔄 **Other Important Rules**

| Situation                     | Overloading Supported?  | Notes                                                            |
| ----------------------------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Changing number of parameters | ✅ Yes                   | Most common form of overloading                                  |
| Changing type of parameters   | ✅ Yes                   | Valid                                                            |
| Changing order of parameters  | ✅ Yes                   | Only if types are different                                      |
| Changing only return type     | ❌ No                    | Not allowed                                                      |
| Changing only parameter names | ❌ No                    | Names ignored during compilation                                 |
| Static methods                | ✅ Yes                   | Can be overloaded                                                |
| Main method                   | ✅ Yes                   | Can be overloaded, but only `main(String[] args)` is entry point |
| Abstract methods              | ✅ Yes                   | As long as parameter list is different                           |
| Private methods               | ✅ Yes                   | Overloading works regardless of access modifier                  |
| Var-args + overloading        | ✅ Yes, but with caution | Only one var-args method per overload group is best practice     |

---

## ✅ Example: All Valid Overloads Together

```java
class OverloadExample {
    void show() {}                        // 1
    void show(int a) {}                   // 2
    void show(String s) {}                // 3
    void show(int a, String s) {}         // 4
    void show(String s, int a) {}         // 5
}
```

---

## ❗ Example: Invalid Overloading

```java
class Test {
    int test(int a) { return a; }
    double test(int a) { return a; } // ❌ Only return type changed
}
```

---

## 🧠 Summary:

| Case                                  | Supported? |
| ------------------------------------- | ---------- |
| Different number of parameters        | ✅ Yes      |
| Different types of parameters         | ✅ Yes      |
| Different order of parameters         | ✅ Yes      |
| Only return type changed              | ❌ No       |
| Only parameter names changed          | ❌ No       |
| Method overloading for static methods | ✅ Yes      |
| Method overloading for constructors   | ✅ Yes      |
| Overloading with var-args             | ✅ Yes      |

