## ✅ **4 Ways to Access a Class (Static) Method**

### 1️⃣ **Directly (from within the same class)**

> ✅ Recommended

```java
class Demo {
    static void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        greet();  // ✔ Direct call
    }
}
```

---

### 2️⃣ **Using the Class Name**

> ✅ Recommended and Best Practice

```java
class Demo {
    static void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Demo.greet();  // ✔ Using class name
    }
}
```

> 🔸 Best way because it clearly shows it's a **class-level** method, not object-level.

---

### 3️⃣ **Using an Object**

> ⚠️ Works, but **Not Recommended**

```java
class Demo {
    static void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Demo obj = new Demo();
        obj.greet();  // ⚠ Works, but not a good practice
    }
}
```

> 🔴 Misleading: It **looks like** you're calling an instance method, but it's a static one.

---

### 4️⃣ **Using an Object Reference**

> ⚠️ Also works, but **Not Recommended**

This is similar to #3. You can use a reference variable to call the method, like:

```java
class Demo {
    static void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Demo obj = new Demo();
        Demo ref = obj;
        ref.greet();  // ⚠ Works, but confusing
    }
}
```

---

## ❌ Why #3 and #4 Are **Not Recommended**:

| Reason                     | Explanation                                                                                           |
| -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Misleading Syntax          | Looks like you are calling an **object (instance)** method.                                           |
| Poor Code Clarity          | Confuses readers – may think it's an instance method.                                                 |
| Bad Practice               | Violates **OOP principles** – class methods should belong to class only.                              |
| Compiler Warning (in IDEs) | Many IDEs (like IntelliJ/Eclipse) show a warning: "Static method should be accessed in a static way." |

---

## ✅ Best Practice Summary

| Way of Access              | Recommended? | Why?                    |
| -------------------------- | ------------ | ----------------------- |
| Direct (within same class) | ✅ Yes        | Clear and concise       |
| Using Class Name           | ✅ Yes        | Clean and best practice |
| Using Object               | ❌ No         | Works but misleading    |
| Using Object Reference     | ❌ No         | Unclear; not good style |

---

## 🧠 Interview Tip:

> **Q: Can we access static methods using an object in Java?**
> **A:** Yes, but it's not recommended. Always use the class name for clarity.

